<section id="leadership-philosophy" class="scroll-mt-24">
  <h2 class="section-label mt-12 mb-2">How I run things</h2>
  <p class="text-muted mt-0 mb-6 max-w-[62ch] text-[14px]">
    Five themes across hiring, decisions, communication, conflict, and AI-augmented engineering. Ask
    the chatbot to dig deeper into any of them.
  </p>

  <div class="max-w-[62ch] space-y-5">
    <p>
      <strong>Hiring.</strong> I hire the person, not the resume. Curiosity matters more than individual
      experiences on a CV — we're always working with unknowns, and the candidate needs to be comfortable
      not knowing everything while having a solid framework for finding their way through. The red flag
      I trust most is over-confidence: superlatives like "X is the best LLM for Y," inability to discuss
      trade-offs, or pure terseness in the conversation itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Decision-making.</strong> I lead with evidence. Agentic coding has made research spikes
      and benchmarks cheap enough that I'd rather burn an afternoon on data than a meeting on opinions.
      When the team is split and the data doesn't decide, I run a 5-whys to surface the actual disagreement.
      Most of the time, defer to the person closest to the domain — unless there's a smell I recognize
      from past experience. The decision pattern I still get wrong: I'm eager to release. Knowing when
      the turkey needs more time is a muscle I'm still working on.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Communication.</strong> Clarity is the cardinal virtue. In tickets, I make it explicit what's
      already decided versus what needs research — and when research is the task, that's part of the work,
      not friction blocking it. Proactive beats reactive: earlier is better, overcommunicating beats undercommunicating.
      The habit I wish more engineers had is making it clear when they're venting versus asking a real
      question. Teams treat ambiguous venting as a real problem, and the cost adds up.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Conflict &amp; feedback.</strong> When tensions are high, I start with personal respect
      — we only critique the work, not the person. If a line has been crossed, I apologize first. Once
      emotions are down, we listen, learn, share, and most of the time we can fall back to an objective
      problem-solving framework and move on. Hard feedback gets prepared in advance: positive, then constructive,
      then action items, then next steps. Unclear feedback is the worst thing a manager can do.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Leading through AI.</strong> The basics still apply, but each engineer now has agents to
      delegate to. Our PR review is two-step — agent first (Claude or Copilot), human second — so the
      human reviewer is reading second-pass code, not first-pass. The new failure mode is "AI slop": novel-length
      PR comments, code that explains the obvious, implementations that miss the mark. The leadership
      skill that matters more now than three years ago is delegating the process of figuring out <em
        >what</em
      > to do, not just how. Agents belong in preparation, not just execution.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
